{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a strong upward trend followed by a flat or slightly declining sentiment score (e.g., Example 1), the model significantly underestimates the upside potential and fails to capture volatility spikes; apply a positive bias correction of +3% to +5% on the forecasted path.",
    "When sentiment is consistently zero while price history exhibits high volatility or recent lows, the model produces an overly smooth, linearly increasing forecast that ignores mean reversion risks; reduce the forecast slope by 40-60% and introduce a downward correction if the last 5 days were below the 20-day average.",
    "The model consistently overestimates the duration of low-price phases when sentiment briefly spikes (e.g., Example 3, day 5) before crashing; apply a negative correction to the forecasted path once sentiment exceeds 0.40 for more than two consecutive days.",
    "In volatile regimes with mixed sentiment signals, the model fails to account for sudden mean reversion toward historical averages; if the true outcome deviates >8% from the forecast within the first 10 days, reverse the trend direction in subsequent forecasts.",
    "When the last 3 days of history are below the 20-day moving average and sentiment is low, the model's linear extrapolation leads to a severe undershoot; apply an immediate upward correction equal to the distance between the current price and the 10-day exponential moving average."
  ]
}
